+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event: editors submit commands over HTTPS, the server assigns each committed edit one event revision, and connected browsers display the revisioned board. How committed revisions reach those browsers is a separate choice from how the board decides them, and it shapes three downstream components at once — the change store (what it must retain and how it hands changes off), delivery (what it serves and what sessions it holds), and browser sync (how a browser subscribes and recovers).
+
+The load is a busy market with up to 800 simultaneous browsers and up to 10–25 editors, averaging two status changes per minute with bursts of up to 20 edits per second around opening time. Committed changes must appear in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, and a browser that was disconnected for up to 20 minutes must be able to recover the latest board. Subsecond updates would be useful but are not required.
+
+Getting this wrong is expensive to unwind: each option implies a different change-store shape, a different delivery surface, and different browser recovery logic, so a reversal after the downstream nodes are built is a rewrite of the delivery half of the system, not a config change.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The 15-second freshness target and the 20-minute recovery requirement are **background, not elements**: every option below can be built to meet them, so they do not drive selection. The same holds for the tenet that a committed edit has one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route — the revision is assigned server-side before delivery under all three options.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Vendor independence** *(major)* — whether the board can deliver changes without depending on an external realtime provider's service, pricing, and API. No tenet mandates this; the project's infrastructure tenet holds self-hosted and managed infrastructure equally permitted. It is weighed major here as a standing concern for a pilot that does not want its delivery path owned by a third party.
+- **Recurring cost** *(major)* — ongoing fees that scale with connections or messages, distinct from the fixed cost of infrastructure the project already runs.
+- **Update latency** *(moderate)* — how quickly a committed change reaches a connected browser. Moderate because the 15s target is the requirement and subsecond is explicitly only "useful."
+- **Operational burden** *(moderate)* — connection capacity planning, reconnect handling, and the session state the board must carry. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+- **Read load** *(minor)* — repeated work the delivery path generates independent of whether the board changed, e.g. 800 browsers re-reading on a fixed interval.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers re-request the board on a fixed ~5-second interval over the existing HTTPS command boundary. The change store exposes cursor queries ("give me revisions after N"); delivery stays plain request/response with no held sessions; browsers run a polling loop and advance a cursor. No long-lived connections exist anywhere.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — runs entirely on the board's own HTTPS surface, no external provider.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no per-connection or per-message fees.
+- Update latency: **partial** — a change surfaces within one poll interval, so up to several seconds of lag; within the 15s target but the slowest of the three.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless request/response, no connection capacity or reconnect logic to own.
+- Read load: **weak** — 800 browsers polling every 5 seconds generate continuous repeated reads whether or not the board changed.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest, lowest-burden option, paid for in repeated reads and a few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The board runs its own WebSocket connection tier. Browsers hold a persistent connection; delivery manages those sessions and pushes committed revisions as they are published; the change store keeps replayable revisions and a reliable publication path (an outbox) so a revision is delivered once and can be replayed on catch-up. Browsers detect drops, reconnect, and request the revisions they missed while disconnected.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — no external realtime provider; the board owns the delivery path end to end.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no third-party per-connection or per-message fees; cost is the infrastructure the project already operates.
+- Update latency: **strong** — revisions are pushed as they commit, giving low-latency updates well inside the target.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the board must plan connection capacity for ~800 simultaneous sockets, carry session state, and own reconnect/catch-up correctness, which is the heaviest operations load of the three.
+- Read load: **strong** — browsers receive pushes rather than re-reading on an interval; steady-state reads track actual changes.
+
+**Summary.** Low-latency, vendor-free updates bought with the connection-capacity and reconnect operations work the board must own itself.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime provider carries the fan-out. The change store reconciles its publications against the provider; delivery wraps the provider's publish API and holds provider credentials; browsers use the provider's subscription and reconnect model. The provider owns connection capacity and reconnect handling.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — delivery is coupled to one provider's service, API, and availability, and to its subscription model in the browser.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — recurring fees that scale with connections and messages.
+- Update latency: **strong** — purpose-built for low-latency push, comfortably inside the target.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider absorbs connection capacity and reconnect handling; the board's remaining work is publication reconciliation and credentials.
+- Read load: **strong** — push-based; no interval re-reads.
+
+**Summary.** The quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations, paid for in recurring fees and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets is chosen.** It is strong on both major criteria — vendor independence and recurring cost — while matching the hosted service on update latency and read load. Polling is strong on the two major criteria too but only partial on latency and weak on read load, and at 800 browsers the repeated-read load is a standing cost for lag the push options avoid. The hosted service matches on latency and read load but is weak on exactly the two criteria weighted major, which is what rules it out: the recurring fees and the coupling of the board's delivery path to a single provider are the costs this decision is least willing to take on for a pilot.
+
+The option is weak on operational burden — owning connection capacity for ~800 sockets plus reconnect and catch-up correctness — and that is the deliberate price. Because neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden was the overriding criterion, trading higher operations work for vendor independence and no recurring fees is consistent with how the criteria are weighted.
+
+This decision cites no tenet. The infrastructure tenet is neutral between self-hosted and managed, so the vendor-independence weighing here is specific to delivery rather than a project-wide lean; if the project wants that preference to govern future choices it has not reached, that would be a new tenet, not something this decision establishes.
+
+**What it commits downstream.** The change store must retain replayable event revisions (the 7-day retention goal already requires retention; this adds replayability) and provide a reliable publication path so each committed revision is published once and can be replayed. Delivery must manage WebSocket sessions and publish revisions to connected browsers. Browser sync must maintain its connection, detect drops against the tenet that connectivity failures stay visible, and catch up on missed revisions after a reconnect within the 20-minute recovery window. It forecloses leaning on a provider to absorb connection scaling: capacity planning for opening-time bursts is now the board's own responsibility.
