 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections the board hosts itself. Five-second HTTPS polling (simplest and stateless, but repeated reads and a few seconds of lag) and a hosted realtime service (fastest to low latency with less connection ops, but recurring fees and provider coupling) were rejected: self-hosted sockets give fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor, accepting the connection-capacity and reconnect operations work in return. This sizes the change store (replayable revisions plus a publication path), delivery (socket session management), and browser sync (reconnect and catch-up). Full rationale, options, and criteria: `docs/decisions/websocket-delivery.md`.
